\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Nocturnal boundary layer transitions present a notorious modeling challenge due to the highly non-linear, non-equilibrium coupling between atmospheric dynamics, surface energy budgets, and microphysical turbulence scales \citep{Mahrt1999, vanDeWiel2017}. This work provides a rigorous Geometric Singular Perturbation Theory (GSPT; \citealp{Fenichel1979, Kuehn2015}) characterization of these transitions, mapping the stable boundary layer (SBL) state-space trajectories as constrained flows on a low-dimensional manifold.

The manuscript is organized as a unified applied-mathematics stack: governing system, fast--slow decomposition, critical manifold geometry, regularization, regime-transition geometry, and numerical experiments.

\section{Scope, Contribution, and Model Positioning}
\label{sec:scope_contribution_positioning}

\subsection{What GSPT-SBL Is and Is Not}
To avoid category errors, we explicitly distinguish the mathematical framework from a full atmospheric turbulence closure suite:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{GSPT-SBL is a reduced fast--slow dynamical systems framework} for regime transitions, manifold geometry, and fold-mediated pathways.
    \item \textbf{GSPT-SBL is not a complete SBL parameterization} intended to resolve all turbulent processes, mesoscale interactions, or replace LES in all regimes.
    \item The intended scientific role is to expose transition topology and timescale separation structure that can then inform closure design and numerics.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Primary Theorem-Level Contribution}
The central mathematical claim can be summarized in theorem form.

\begin{assumption}[Regularity and Separation Conditions]
Assume smooth vector fields for the coupled fast--slow SBL system, finite nonzero timescale separation parameter $\varepsilon$, and physically admissible parameter ranges under which the critical set is piecewise smooth and compact in the analyzed domain.
\end{assumption}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric Transition Structure of GSPT-SBL]
Under the stated regularity and separation assumptions, the regularized fast--slow system admits a normally hyperbolic slow manifold away from fold singularities. Regime transitions are organized by folded critical manifold geometry and associated branch switching pathways, rather than by discontinuous closure switching rules.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
In this manuscript, the theorem is supported by analytical construction where available and by numerical manifold diagnostics where closed-form expressions are not tractable.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Structural vs Calibrated Parameters}
For interpretability and transferability, parameters are partitioned into:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Structural parameters:} quantities controlling model topology and fast--slow geometry (for example $\varepsilon$, $\delta$, manifold-shaping coefficients, regularization scales).
    \item \textbf{Calibration parameters:} physically tuned exchange, radiative, and surface-coupling coefficients tied to site, forcing, roughness, or campaign context.
\end{itemize}
This separation clarifies which conclusions are intrinsic to the framework and which are dataset dependent.

\subsection{Operational Definition of Turbulence Collapse}
Throughout this work, ``collapse'' is used in an operational dynamical sense: concurrent decay of turbulence energy and transport efficacy below prescribed thresholds, accompanied by state-space transition toward a weak-mixing attracting branch. This definition distinguishes geometric regime transition from transient amplitude fluctuations.

As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:gspt_paradigm_pipeline}, the GSPT framework replaces discontinuous algebraic switching with dynamic passage through regularized folded manifold geometry, making the transition architecture explicit before introducing the full fast--slow equation set.

