\section{Structural Stability, Falsifiable Predictions, and Limitations}
\label{sec:stability_predictions_limitations}

\subsection{Structural Stability Considerations}
The practical utility of the framework depends on persistence of the folded geometry under moderate perturbations to forcing and parameter values. While a full structural-stability proof over the complete forcing class is outside present scope, numerical continuation and parameter sweeps indicate that the principal branch architecture and fold-mediated transition pathways are robust over the analyzed regime envelope.

\subsection{Falsifiable Predictions}
The framework yields testable claims that can be rejected by data:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Hysteresis under forcing cycles:} collapse and recovery thresholds need not coincide under cyclic cooling/warming trajectories.
    \item \textbf{Branch occupancy signatures:} trajectory residence time should cluster near identifiable manifold sectors during persistent stable regimes.
    \item \textbf{Recovery asymmetry:} post-collapse recovery pathways should differ dynamically from pre-collapse cooling pathways in phase-space coordinates.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Practical Implications for Atmospheric Modeling}
For SCM and NWP closure development, the main gain is not wholesale model replacement but topology-aware closure logic:
\begin{itemize}
    \item fewer ad hoc stability switches,
    \item smoother numerical behavior near regime transition,
    \item physically interpretable transition diagnostics,
    \item incremental integration into existing model stacks.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and Domain of Validity}
We emphasize the following scope boundaries:
\begin{itemize}
    \item focus on nocturnal stable boundary-layer dynamics,
    \item reduced-order representation of intermittency and wave-coupled processes,
    \item limited treatment of cloud-radiative feedbacks, complex terrain, and heterogeneous canopies,
    \item framework-level interpretation rather than universal closure replacement.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion: A Geometric Framework for Stable Boundary-Layer Dynamics}
\label{sec:conclusion}

By resolving the fast--slow geometry of nocturnal boundary-layer evolution, this work demonstrates that the abrupt decay of turbulent mixing can be understood as a boundary-mediated transition on a low-dimensional invariant manifold rather than solely through empirical similarity relations. The resulting $4\text{D}$ GSPT-SBL framework provides a geometrically consistent bridge between non-equilibrium fluid dynamics and atmospheric boundary-layer theory while offering an alternative explanation for persistent limitations of Monin--Obukhov Similarity Theory (MOST; \citealp{nieuwstadt1984}) under strongly stable conditions.

\subsection{Mathematical and Topological Rigor}
The framework formally distinguishes between two physical transitions previously conflated in meteorological literature:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Transversal Boundary Crossing ($e = 0$):} The isolated fast TKE subsystem undergoes a deterministic recovery pathway along the boundary of physical phase space.
    \item \textbf{Emergent Fold Catastrophe ($\mathcal{C}_{\text{fold}}$):} An $S$-shaped fold catastrophe is demonstrated analytically to be an emergent phenomenon occurring exclusively when the boundary layer is coupled to the nonlinear Surface Energy Budget (SEB).
\end{enumerate}
This distinction clarifies the origin of the ``Fold Illusion'' and provides a first-principles framework necessary to describe intermittent turbulence and relaxation oscillations without relying on ad-hoc empirical switching functions \citep{zilitinkevich2007, basu2008}.

\subsection{Physical Realism and the Nocturnal Lifecycle}
The coupled dynamical system naturally generates a four-phase nocturnal lifecycle consisting of Evening Cooling, Catastrophic Collapse, Inertial Acceleration, and Turbulent Breakout, forming a closed relaxation oscillation. Crucially, the Low-Level Jet (LLJ) is no longer prescribed phenomenologically; it emerges naturally as the Blackadar inertial response along the laminar manifold sheet following frictional decoupling. The introduction of an adaptive emergent scale height ($h_{\text{eff}}$) further ensures that SBL depth is a dynamic property of state-space trajectories, substantially mitigating the runaway cooling tendencies encountered in static local closures \citep{Derbyshire1990, Holtslag1993}.

\subsection{Observational Validation and the RMSE Paradox}
Field campaign comparisons across CASES-99 \citep{poulos2002cases99}, FLOSS, and SHEBA highlight a geometric paradox where standard error metrics (RMSE) act as a proxy for frictional regularization. Rather than treating RMSE solely as a measure of model deficiency, the manifold framework suggests that part of the observed error reflects physically meaningful exploration of nonlinear state space. The framework explains why higher errors in open terrain (CASES-99) correspond to unconstrained global dynamics exploring non-linear phase space, whereas high-roughness canopy or snowpack environments (FLOSS) act as dynamic dampers that pin trajectories tightly to the manifold.

\subsection{Numerical Innovation and 5D Autonomous Extensions}
Implemented in the modern Julia/SciML ecosystem, the framework achieves a $\sim 20\times$ computational efficiency gain through Banded Jacobian prototypes when evaluated against dense benchmarks, decoupling solver overhead from vertical grid resolution. The Discrete Exterior Calculus (DEC) formulation is designed to preserve discrete geometric structure and improve global conservation properties, reducing susceptibility to unphysical numerical artifacts during rapid transitions.

To explain intermittent turbulence natively, future work will extend this $4\text{D}$ construction into a $5\text{D}$ autonomous ODE system by promoting potential temperature ($\theta$) to an autonomous slow dynamical variable. This $5\text{D}$ extension represents the minimal mathematical setting required to support canard-mediated transitions \citep{krupa2001extending, desroches2012canards} and mixed-mode oscillations (MMOs) natively, unifying deterministic and intermittent SBL regimes under a single topological paradigm.

\subsection{Applied Horizons: Offshore Wind and Marine Boundary Layers}
Beyond terrestrial SBLs, the geometric mechanisms established here have direct implications for marine atmospheric boundary layers (MABL). In offshore environments, low surface roughness and intense marine thermal stratification frequently give rise to persistent low-level wind shear and decoupling. Characterizing these dynamic transitions addresses a major open hurdle in offshore wind energy resource estimation and turbine fatigue modeling \citep{shaw2022grand}, demonstrating the operational applicability of the GSPT framework to complex marine environments.

\subsection{Synthesis and Outlook}
The geometric viewpoint developed here suggests that stable boundary-layer dynamics are governed not by isolated empirical stability corrections but by the global organization of trajectories on a coupled fast--slow manifold. Within this framework, turbulent collapse, intermittent recovery, inertial oscillations, and surface-energy feedback emerge as interconnected manifestations of a single nonlinear dynamical system. By combining geometric singular perturbation theory with physically consistent boundary-layer physics, the proposed framework establishes a mathematically rigorous foundation for future turbulence parameterizations while providing a pathway toward more robust and physically interpretable weather and climate models.

